# ADR 145: Enforce Authorization Checks at Controller Entry Points

## Context

Controller methods are the entry points for HTTP requests and must validate that the authenticated user has permission to access or modify the requested resource before executing any business logic.

## Decision

All controller methods that access or modify domain resources MUST perform authorization checks before executing business logic.

## Policies

1. All controller methods that access or modify domain resources MUST perform authorization checks before executing business logic

## Implementation Guidelines

- Authorization checks must occur at the start of the controller method, before any service calls
- Use a consistent authorization service or middleware pattern across all controllers
- Authorization failures must return appropriate HTTP status codes (403 Forbidden for insufficient permissions, 401 Unauthorized for unauthenticated requests)
- Log authorization failures for security auditing
- Do not rely solely on frontend validation or service-layer checks

## Examples

### ✅ Correct

```typescript
@Post('/users/:id')
async updateUser(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const userId = req.params.id;
  const currentUser = req.user;
  
  // Authorization check FIRST
  if (!await authService.canUpdateUser(currentUser, userId)) {
    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
  }
  
  // Then execute business logic
  const user = await userService.updateUser(userId, req.body);
  res.json(user);
}
```

### ❌ Incorrect

```typescript
@Post('/users/:id')
async updateUser(req: Request, res: Response) {
  // Business logic executed without authorization check
  const user = await userService.updateUser(req.params.id, req.body);
  res.json(user);
}
```
